{"type": "messages", "messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are the isolated source-summarizer worker in Research World. Obey the supplied evidence and role boundary. Return one JSON object and no prose."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Summarize each supplied source without exceeding its scope. Preserve source_id, artifact_id, sha256, URL, and line locator. Return {\"summaries\":[{\"source_id\":str,\"summary\":str,\"limits\":str}]} covering every source.\nINPUT:\n{\"direction\": {\"title\": \"Computational asymmetry between primality testing and integer factorization\", \"rationale\": \"Synthesize theoretical computer science literature to clarify why deterministic primality verification resides in P while exact factorization remains computationally intractable.\", \"workflow\": \"evidence_synthesis\", \"completion_test\": \"A structured review explicitly maps algorithmic classes, cites peer-reviewed complexity proofs, and distinguishes worst-case from average-case hardness.\", \"remaining\": \"Engineering teams must choose which hardness assumptions align with current hardware capabilities and threat models for deployment.\", \"position\": 2}, \"sources\": [{\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:8bc5295a2d4242bcc2624f816ea29b0d3f7d56401a62167c588ee6bc5ddaafd6\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"sha256\": \"af0044a18d0ca8e3a261765db22b9675dbeeaacd2322089a3b6feff0e50765a6\", \"title\": \"Why is not known whether integer factorization can be done in polynomial time knowing how to do primality tests efficiently?\", \"url\": \"https://cs.stackexchange.com/questions/50767/why-is-not-known-whether-integer-factorization-can-be-done-in-polynomial-time-kn\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_upstream_error\\nTarget returned an unsupported response.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:1c996ca287c8403a9c5e68f103999611dd6f5829546e45255636a3f087f180e6\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:1403795b45a08ab6e3a7983ba5c5b5bd4a8ffdb6026d6583a760b19a7d8a9c06\", \"sha256\": \"1403795b45a08ab6e3a7983ba5c5b5bd4a8ffdb6026d6583a760b19a7d8a9c06\", \"title\": \"AKS primality test\", \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 341}, \"content\": \"## AKS primality test - Wikipedia\\n\\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test\\n\\n---\\n\\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#bodyContent)\\n\\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\\n\\nAlgorithm checking for prime numbers\\n\\nThe**AKS primality test**(also known as the**Agrawal–Kayal–Saxena primality test**and the**cyclotomic AKS test**) is\\na[deterministic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)[primality-\\nproving](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_test)[algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithm)created and published by[Manindra\\nAgrawal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manindra_Agrawal),[Neeraj Kayal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neeraj_Kayal), and[Nitin\\nSaxena](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitin_Saxena), computer scientists at the[Indian Institute of Technology\\nKanpur](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_Institute_of_Technology_Kanpur), on August 6, 2002, in an article titled \\\"PRIMES is in\\nP\\\".[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)The algorithm was the first one which is able to determine in[polynomial\\ntime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time), whether a given number\\nis[prime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prime_number)or[composite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Composite_number)without relying on[mathematical\\nconjectures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_conjecture)such as the[generalized Riemann\\nhypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis). The proof is also notable for not relying on the field\\nof[analysis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Analysis_(mathematics)).[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-2)In 2006 the authors\\nreceived both the[Gödel Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del_Prize)and[Fulkerson Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulkerson_Prize)for their work.\\n\\n## Importance[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=1)]\\n\\nAKS is the first primality-proving algorithm to be simultaneously*general*,*polynomial-time*,*deterministic*, and*unconditionally correct*. Previous algorithms\\nhad been developed for centuries and achieved three of these properties at most, but not all four.\\n- The AKS algorithm can be used to verify the primality of any**general**number given. Many fast primality tests are known that work only for numbers with\\ncertain properties. For example, the[Lucas–Lehmer test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas%E2%80%93Lehmer_primality_test)works only for[Mersenne\\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mersenne_number), while[Pépin's test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/P%C3%A9pin%27s_test)can be applied to[Fermat\\nnumbers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat_number)only.\\n- The maximum running time of the algorithm can be bounded by a**[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_time#Polynomial_time)**over the number of\\ndigits in the target number.[ECPP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptic_curve_primality_proving)and[APR](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adleman%E2%80%93Pomeran\\nce%E2%80%93Rumely_primality_test)conclusively prove or disprove that a given number is prime, but are not known to have polynomial time bounds for all inputs.\\n- The algorithm is guaranteed to distinguish**[deterministically](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_algorithm)**whether the target number is prime or\\ncomposite. Randomized tests, such as[Miller–Rabin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test)and[Baillie–PSW](https://en.wikipedia.org/w\\niki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test), can test any given number for primality in polynomial time, but are known to produce only a probabilistic result.\\n- The correctness of AKS is**not conditional**on any subsidiary unproved[hypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hypothesis). In contrast, Miller's version of\\nthe[Miller–Rabin test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miller%E2%80%93Rabin_primality_test#Deterministic_variants)is fully deterministic and runs in polynomial\\ntime over all inputs, but its correctness depends on the truth of the yet-unproved[generalized Riemann\\nhypothesis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_Riemann_hypothesis).\\n\\nWhile the algorithm is of immense theoretical importance, it is not used in practice, rendering it a[galactic\\nalgorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Galactic_algorithm). For 64-bit inputs, the[Baillie–PSW\\ntest](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baillie%E2%80%93PSW_primality_test)is deterministic and runs many orders of magnitude faster. For larger inputs, the\\nperformance of the (also unconditionally correct) ECPP and APR tests is*far*superior to AKS. Additionally, ECPP can output a[primality\\ncertificate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primality_certificate)that allows independent and rapid verification of the results, which is not possible with the\\nAKS algorithm.\\n\\n## Concepts[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=AKS_primality_test&action=edit&section=2)]\\n\\nThe AKS primality test is based upon the following theorem: Given an integern≥2{\\\\displaystyle n\\\\geq 2}and integera{\\\\displaystyle\\na}[coprime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coprime)ton{\\\\displaystyle n},n{\\\\displaystyle n}is prime if and only if\\nthe[polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)[congruence\\nrelation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Congruence_relation#Modular_arithmetic)(X+a)n≡Xn+a(modn){\\\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}\\\\equiv X^{n}+a{\\\\pmod {n}}}1\\n\\nholds within the polynomial ring(Z/nZ)[X]{\\\\displaystyle (\\\\mathbb {Z} /n\\\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-\\nAKS-1)Note thatX{\\\\displaystyle X}denotes the[indeterminate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring#Definition_(univariate_case))which generates this\\npolynomial ring.\\n\\nThis theorem is a generalization to polynomials of[Fermat's little theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fermat's_little_theorem). In one direction it can\\neasily be proved using the[binomial theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_theorem)together with the following property of the[binomial\\ncoefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient):(nk)≡0(modn){\\\\displaystyle {n \\\\choose k}\\\\equiv 0{\\\\pmod {n}}}for all0<k<n{\\\\displaystyle\\n0<k<n}ifn{\\\\displaystyle n}is prime.\\n\\nWhile the relation (**[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#math_1)**) constitutes a primality test in itself, verifying it takes[exponential\\ntime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_time): the[brute force](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute_force_method)approach would require the expansion of\\nthe(X+a)n{\\\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}}polynomial and a reduction(modn){\\\\displaystyle {\\\\pmod {n}}}of the resultingn+1{\\\\displaystyle n+1}coefficients.\\n\\nThe congruence is an equality in the[polynomial ring](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial_ring)(Z/nZ)[X]{\\\\displaystyle (\\\\mathbb {Z} /n\\\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}.\\nEvaluating in a quotient ring of(Z/nZ)[X]{\\\\displaystyle (\\\\mathbb {Z} /n\\\\mathbb {Z} )[X]}creates an upper bound for the degree of the polynomials involved. The\\nAKS evaluates the equality in(Z/nZ)[X]/(Xr−1){\\\\displaystyle (\\\\mathbb {Z} /n\\\\mathbb {Z} )[X]/(X^{r}-1)}, making the[computational\\ncomplexity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_complexity_theory)dependent on the size ofr{\\\\displaystyle r}. For\\nclarity,[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AKS_primality_test#cite_note-AKS-1)this is expressed as the congruence(X+a)n≡(Xn+a)(modXr−1,n){\\\\displaystyle\\n(X+a)^{n}\\\\equiv (X^{n}+a){\\\\pmod {X^{r}-1,n}}}2\\n\\nwhich is the same as:(X+a)n−(Xn+a)=(Xr−1)g+nf{\\\\displaystyle (X+a)^{n}-(X^{n}+a)=(X^{r}-1)g+nf}3\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:0f69af372ba3c221eed6e469885a0b96b8901ba513c8935cd11bd6b72d8b6c4e\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:e6d74098c2706366bcc343ac26fee7cbaa111844e0642556bc42327d03189e7f\", \"sha256\": \"e6d74098c2706366bcc343ac26fee7cbaa111844e0642556bc42327d03189e7f\", \"title\": \"Primality Testing in Polynomial Time: From Randomized Algorithms to \\\"PRIMES Is in P\\\" | Springer Nature Link\", \"url\": \"https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 8}, \"content\": \"## Client Challenge\\n\\n**Source**: https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/b12334\\n\\n---\\n\\nA required part of this site couldn’t load. This may be due to a browser extension, network issues, or browser settings. Please check your connection, disable\\nany ad blockers, or try using a different browser.\"}, {\"source_id\": \"source-snapshot:a4961ccca36b97c4722a89f5ee273d468a89d9f74e225a8a67cd42e1526ea03a\", \"artifact_id\": \"artifact:5b76592e66de71aaf6613906161aa86db5bd641a862e8ecdb21efe5035d6df5b\", \"sha256\": \"5b76592e66de71aaf6613906161aa86db5bd641a862e8ecdb21efe5035d6df5b\", \"title\": \"Identifying primes in polynomial time: the AKS algorithm\", \"url\": \"https://academicweb.nd.edu/~andyp/notes/AKS.pdf\", \"locator\": {\"line_start\": 1, \"line_end\": 2}, \"content\": \"extract_unsupported_content\\nResponse content type is not supported.\"}]}"}]}
